Yes — **a student pilot permit holder may act as pilot-in-command** on a **daytime VFR training flight in Canada with no passengers on board**, **provided all conditions in CAR 401.19(1) are met**.

Under **CAR 401.19(1)**, the holder may act as PIC of an aircraft of the endorsed category if:

- the flight is **for the purpose of the holder’s flight training**;
- the flight is **conducted in Canada**;
- the flight is **conducted under day VFR**;
- the flight is **conducted under the direction and supervision of a person qualified to provide training** toward the permit, licence or rating for which the PIC experience is required; and
- **no passenger is carried on board**.

So the answer is **yes**, but **only if** it is a supervised training flight meeting all of those conditions.

### Important exception
Under **CAR 401.19(2)**, a student pilot permit holder **enrolled in an integrated course** may act as PIC on a flight conducted under **day or night VFR**, despite the day-VFR limitation in paragraph 401.19(1)(c).

Under **CAR 401.34(1)**, **subject to subsection (2)**, the holder of an **airline transport pilot licence — aeroplane** may exercise the privileges of:

- a **private pilot licence — aeroplane**; and
- a **commercial pilot licence — aeroplane**.

From the provided sections, that means the ATPL(A) holder may, as applicable to the ratings held:

- act as **pilot-in-command or co-pilot** of an aeroplane of a class and type endorsed on the licence (**401.26(a)**, read with **401.34(1)** and **401.39**);
- act as **pilot-in-command of an ultra-light aeroplane** (**401.26(b)**);
- act as **pilot-in-command or co-pilot** for the sole purpose of the holder’s **flight training or flight test**, subject to the conditions in **401.26(c)**;
- exercise the privileges of a **VFR OTT rating** (**401.30(1)(b)**);
- while engaged in providing a **commercial air service** by aeroplane of a class and type for which the licence is endorsed with ratings, act as:
  - **pilot-in-command** if the minimum flight crew document specifies **one pilot**, or
  - **co-pilot** (**401.30(1)(c)**);
- if qualified as a flight instructor in accordance with **section 425.21 of Standard 425**, **conduct flight instruction** (**401.30(1)(d)**);
- exercise **private pilot licence — aeroplane privileges** until the end of the medical validity period specified for the private pilot licence (**401.30(1)(e)**).

## Important limitation in subsection (2)
Under **CAR 401.34(2)**, if the holder’s ATPL(A) is **endorsed with a Group 1 instrument rating**, the holder may, **while engaged in providing a commercial air service** by means of an aeroplane of a class and type for which the licence is endorsed, act as:

- **pilot-in-command**, if the minimum flight crew document specifies a minimum flight crew of **two pilots** (**401.34(2)(a)**); or
- **co-pilot** (**401.34(2)(b)**).

So, in short, **401.34(1)** gives the holder the privileges of both the **PPL(A)** and **CPL(A)**, and **401.34(2)** adds the specific ATPL commercial air service privilege for **two-crew PIC** (and co-pilot) operations, where the licence is endorsed with a **Group 1 instrument rating** and the appropriate class/type rating.
